 idea with words for sure but you can  also use facial expressions body  language color light contrast and  hundreds of other techniques that form  the backbone of dozens of genres and  mediums if there's communication the  transmission of ideas then there is  language if the audience doesn't  understand the language  then there's a failure of communication  so this idea that games are weaker  because the audience can fail isn't  actually unique to games because books  painting photography television and  movies can all fail to communicate in  their own ways let's get the most  obvious out of the way spoken or written  language if you don't speak the regional  language a film or book is written in  then you probably won't understand it  oh sure you might get the gist of it for  film at least where the overall language  is so much more than just the spoken  dialogue but if you don't speak 17th  century Spanish then you're probably not  going to get very far into it Ignacio  Hidalgo Don Quixote de la Mancha before  you give up but this extends even deeper  than capital L languages there's a  massive online trend of niche and genre  film book and television reviewers who  are proving that the inherent language  of film isn't as inherent as it seems  what makes a great art film isn't the  same as what makes a great action film  the language of schlocky horror isn't  the same as the language of cheesy  comedy the pacing gags blocking lighting  music and mimetic shorthand are all  different  if you can't adjust to the recursive and  broadly referencing language of  something like adaptation then the movie  is going to be an unwatchable mess if  you don't understand solipsism then the  gnawing uncertainty and madness that  builds throughout broken flowers won't  even register in the entire film will  just look like Bill Murray being sort of  funny in a really really awkward way as  a musical example if you have never  dealt with crushing depression then the  Barenaked Ladies pinch me is just a  catchy tune with some odd lyrics I feel  fine enough I guess  considering and great things amass this  capacity for miscommunication is  probably the most dramatic in the still  mediums a viewer can stare at a painting  or photograph for seconds minutes or  hours completing the mechanical  observation and yet still never  understand what it was they were looking  at or why it was interesting this is  most obvious in social or political art  as these pieces typically require  extensive explanation in order to get a  modern viewer up to speed with the  language of the piece maybe what makes  it interesting isn't all that obvious to  start it's like Andy Warhol's art it's  been around so long at this point that  you have to take a step back to remember  a time when it was special and people  really weren't too sure what to do with  it so yeah context is a factor to all of  this is language that goes into the  communication of the ideas and intense  and without that shared language the  viewer can fail they may stay in the  theater until the credits roll they may  stay awake until the end but they've  still sat and spun their mental tires  for a bit making no progress towards the  actual conclusion failure is no more  unique to videogames than color is to  painting in addition to that the  possibility of vastly different physical  experiences isn't unique either looking  at my ICO example from earlier I was  able to finish the game two different  times 14 hours the first time two hours  the second my sister never finished  compared to film this seems obscene  Chinatown 30 minutes of driving around  pacing you know all that that we talked  about earlier that'd be a pretty damning  point if film was the only narrative  media out there but what about books we  still consider pacing to be important in  literature even though the time it takes  to read a book can be pretty extreme  from person to person it took me almost  10 months to work my way through lame is  the first time I read The Giver it took  me a couple of weeks reading at a half  hour or more at a time the fourth or  fifth time I read it it took me maybe  four hours cover to cover but we still  talk about books as being well or poorly  paced even if it takes one person hours  longer to get through 50 pages than it  takes someone else okay so if there's  all these similarities  between video games and film and  literature and photography and painting  and music then why is there any  controversy at all  well because there are differences but  in my mind this is where we start to get  into the really cool stuff  video games bring in new vectors for  interaction and communication basically  new forms of language that merge the  capabilities of other mediums in video  games the mechanics of playing the gamer  is much a part of the language as the  dialogue music art and pacing one of the  best examples of this is the  first-person shooter now at first glance  it may seem that the first-person games  would be the easiest for new players to  pick up but they're actually one of the  hardest first-person games rely on very  specific conventions in their control  scheme a vocabulary of commands if you  will using one hand to steer the feet  while using the other to steer the head  and torso that kind of spatial  abstraction is surprisingly difficult  for new players to master while  incredibly intuitive for people who  already know it you know like a language  this language element becomes incredibly  obvious if you go back and play the  early versions of complicated genres  like realtime strategy games games like  Warcraft orcs and humans are doomed to  lack many of the control options that  we've become used to and trying to play  them these days is like trying to  explain insanity wolf to your  grandparents it's a communication  problem beyond that the natural  extension of mechanics are the choices  they represent and the consequences of  those choices th